  "names": {
    "order": "given family",
    "conventions": "Given name, patronymic and family name: Oleksandr Petrovych Melnyk, Olena Ivanivna Melnyk. The patronymic (usually -ovych for men, -ivna for women) is used in formal address, while everyday polite address is пане or пані with the first name (пане Олександре). Surnames ending in -enko, -uk, -chuk or a consonant (Shevchenko, Kovalchuk, Melnyk) are the same for men and women; adjective surnames change, so Kovalskyi becomes Kovalska. Short forms are everywhere: Sasha for Oleksandr, Andriiko, Dima, Olia, Natasha, Katia. Latin spellings here follow the official transliteration used in Ukrainian passports. Crimean Tatars carry Turkic and Muslim given names (Server, Elmira) with surnames that take -ova for women (Asanov, Asanova).",
